      "reply": {
        "answer": "Okay, let\u2019s break this down.\n\n**What does engine oil do?**\n\nEngine oil is designed to lubricate, cool and seal internal engine components. [S1] [S2] It\u2019s like a very thin, special grease that flows through your engine. It does several important things:\n\n*   **Lubrication:** It reduces friction and wear between moving parts, preventing them from grinding against each other.\n*   **Cooling:** It helps to cool the engine parts it flows over.\n*   **Sealing:** It helps to seal the gaps between moving parts.\n\n**Why does it need changing?**\n\nRunning your engine with little or no oil will cause the engine to overheat and eventually seize up. [S2] Neglecting to change the oil periodically will shorten the life of your engine. [S2]\n\n**Technical terms explained:**\n\n*   **Engine oil:** A clean or refined form of crude oil, specially formulated to flow easily, reduce friction, and prevent rust and corrosion. [S1]\n*   **Oil pan:** The container at the bottom of the engine that holds the engine oil.\n*   **Dipstick:** A tool used to check the oil level. [S2]\n*   **Gasket:** A thin, flexible material used to seal the oil filter. [S3]\n\n**Simple Example:**\n\nThink of it like this: if you were to slide two pieces of wood together without any lubricant, they would quickly rub together and wear down. Engine oil is like that lubricant, keeping the engine parts from damaging each other.\n\n[S2] provides a step-by-step guide to checking and changing the oil.",
